h of the walls being blown out the  roof would eventually collapse  creating a big pile of rubble that could  kill any enemies still inside  battlefield 3 built on this with the  introduction of micro destruction  so you didn't need grenade launchers or  rockets to break walls  your standard assault rifles would do  the trick if you would chip away at  smaller barricades walls and furniture  eventually you would start noticing the  decay there was a trade-off with this  however  sure more things would be visually  destroyed but it wasn't as significant  and the scale was much smaller leaving  less of the environment to actually be  destructible  battlefield 3 essentially traded away  larger destruction  for better overall looks the solution to  this in my opinion  ended up being the next logical step of  destruction gradual changes to the map  that take effect over the course of an  entire match which is where battlefield  went on to invent  the levolution so first things first  let's talk about the difference between  destruction and levolution  destruction was prevalent in the  battlefield games listed so far  and is basically part of the game's  identity but levolution refers very  specifically to large-scale destruction  events in battlefield 4  which to me is honestly the holy grail  and perfect balance of destruction  the concept on paper is simple each  multiplayer round in battlefield 4  has a sort of timeline attached to the  map so long certain parameters are met  so by the time you're in the latter half  of the round chances are  a major set piece or sometimes huge  portions of the map  would look nothing like it did when you  first started and we're not talking just  small roadways or bridges being removed  i'm talking big-ass skyscrapers in siege  of shanghai that had an objective on the  roof spectacularly collapsing into a  colossal pile of rubble  there are plenty of other areas like  this in other battlefield 4 maps  where as long as specific areas 